{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"valiant-balladic-willet","creation_channel":"cli","description":"# `plan_review/passes.py` is now at the hard cap with zero headroom\n\n## Context\n\nDiscovered while landing c6c9 (the `attest.py` split). With `attest.py` brought down to 656, the largest file in `src/rebar/llm/plan_review/` is now `passes.py` at **exactly 800** lines — the hard cap enforced by `tests/unit/test_module_size_contract.py::test_no_src_module_over_the_hard_cap` (limit single-sourced in `.github/module-size-limit.txt`, gate is `> limit`).\n\nThis is the identical failure mode c6c9 was raised for: the file passes only because the comparison is strictly-greater. **Any** next edit to `passes.py` — including a one-line bug fix — cannot land without first splitting it, and that cost lands on whoever next needs to fix a bug there, under time pressure, as an unrelated blocker.\n\nCurrent top of the package (post-c6c9):\n\n    800  passes.py          <- at the cap, zero headroom\n    784  __init__.py\n    776  pass1.py\n    769  registry.py\n    759  det_floor.py\n    726  sizing.py\n    702  orchestrator.py\n\nNote this is a *cluster* problem, not a single-file one: seven files in this one package sit between 700 and 800. `passes.py` is the urgent one; the rest are a standing risk worth a broader look.\n\n## Approach\n\nSame discipline as c6c9 and 2682 — split ONLY along a seam that already exists in the call graph, never mechanically to hit a number, and never creating a sub-100-LOC file.\n\nThe c6c9 split is the worked reference for the two things that make this safe:\n\n1. **Find the reference set with BOTH tools.** Serena `find_referencing_symbols` AND a repo-wide string `grep` for each moved symbol — the LSP cannot see `monkeypatch.setattr` / `getattr` / `importlib` targets, and an empty semantic result is not trustworthy.\n2. **Watch for patch seams.** In c6c9 the moved code called two helpers that the suite monkeypatches ON the source module object; a module-level `from .x import y` in the new module would have bound the pre-patch objects and silently ignored the patches (verified: it turns 4 tests RED). Function-local imports preserve the semantics. Expect the same class of hazard here.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `src/rebar/llm/plan_review/passes.py` is under 700 LOC\n- [ ] any extracted module is at least 100 LOC\n- [ ] the split follows a pre-existing call-graph seam, with the caller analysis recorded in the commit message\n- [ ] zero test edits are required by the move, or each required edit is justified in the commit message\n- [ ] [operator-attested] the full suite passes with a test count equal to the pre-split count, evidenced by the two pytest summary lines quoted in a comment","id":"8705-5c65-c1b6-42de","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"task","title":"passes.py sits at exactly 800/800 LOC — the next file at the cap"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1785896718414675001,"uuid":"f13c678d-c679-4f7c-8c00-9d3542574c51"}